{
  "moduleId": "m00172",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00172:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Electrophilic Additions to Conjugated Dienes: Allylic Carbocations"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "9ebc3bdf-d157-4311-b829-5470694c9c73"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00172:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "scaled-down unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00172:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "2-methylpropene reacts with hydrogen chloride in ether to yield a tertiary carbocation that further yields 2-chloro-2-methylpropane. 1,4-Pentadiene (nonconjugated) reacts with hydrogen chloride in ether to yield 2,4-dichloropentane."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_14_02_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00172:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "scaled-down unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00172:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "In a reaction, 1,3-butadiene reacts with hydrogen bromide to yield 3-bromo-1-butene (71 percent) through 1,2-addition and 1-bromo-2-butene (29 percent) through 1,4-addition."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_14_02_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00172:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "scaled-down unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00172:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "In a reaction, 1,3-butadiene reacts with bromine at 20 degrees Celsius to yield 3,4-dibromo-1-butene (55 percent) through 1,2-addition and 1,4-dibromo-2-butene (45 percent) through 1,4-addition."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_14_02_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00172:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "scaled-down unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00172:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "A reaction shows 1,3-butadiene reacting with hydrogen bromide to yield two resonance structures of secondary, allylic carbocations. Primary, nonallylic carbocation is not formed."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_14_02_004.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00172:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "scaled-down",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00172:caption:fig-00005-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00172:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "Two resonance structures of allylic carbocations reacts with bromide ion form 1,4-addition (29 percent) and 1,2-addition (71 percent) products. The electrostatic potential map along with ball-and-stick model of carbocation is depicted."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_14_02_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
